  cites Foreword chapter Console

 A page link navigates when the browser is holding the tree that was drawn.

 This is the half `browser-click-dispatch` cannot reach. That test clicks a
 browser straight out of `browser-init`, where `bs-laid` is None, so every
 page click answers code 0 and the address never moves -- which is correct
 behaviour for a browser that has not painted yet, and says nothing about
 links. Here the tree is laid out first and memoized, which is what the desk
 pane does on every repaint.

 Laying out `tab-content` instead would not do, and the difference is the
 defect this test exists for. `widget-layout` builds a fresh WidgetNode
 rather than writing bounds into the one it is given, so the tree the page
 builder returned keeps `layout-rect-zero` on every node however many times
 it is drawn. The scan below reported no navigating y at all before the tree
 was kept.

 The scan runs DOWN the window, so the recording says which band reaches
 which address rather than asserting one hand-picked coordinate. A y that
 does not navigate contributes nothing: the chrome bands and the gaps between
 link rows are expected to be silent.

 Each row builds its OWN state. `__record-set` stores in place and returns
 the same record, so a shared subject would carry row one's navigation into
 row two and the scan would measure the rows in order rather than the rows.

Section: Test

  addr-of : BrowserState -> Text
  addr-of (st) =
    when browser-active-tab st
      is Just (t) -> t.tab-address
      is None -> "(none)"

  memoized : Integer, Integer -> BrowserState
  memoized (w) (h) =
    let st = browser-init w h browser-theme-default display-init-text-fallback
    in let mark = __heap-save
    in let laid = widget-layout (browser-render st) (st.bs-theme) (layout-rect 0 0 w h)
    in browser-memo-laid st laid mark

  probe : Integer, Integer -> Text
  probe (mx) (my) =
    let st = memoized 1024 768
    in let after = browser-handle-mouse-click st mx my
    in let a = addr-of after
    in if a == "codex://local/newtab" then "" else " " & integer-to-text my & "=" & a

  scan : Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  scan (mx) (y) (limit) (acc) =
    if y >= limit then acc
    else scan mx (y + 16) limit (acc & probe mx y)

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("x=200" & scan 200 64 448 "")
    print-line-uni ("x=20" & scan 20 64 448 "")
  end
